1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:05,010
Spaceflight has killed 21 people. It came within seconds of killing 32 more.

2
00:00:05,010 --> 00:00:08,248
And every single one of them made it home.

3
00:00:09,366 --> 00:00:14,002
An astronaut who watched flaming chunks of his own ship fly past the window.

4
00:00:14,002 --> 00:00:18,307
A commander who decided he would spend his final seconds insulting mission control.

5
00:00:18,307 --> 00:00:20,625
A man who nearly drowned. In space.

6
00:00:21,766 --> 00:00:24,014
These are not the disasters you know.

7
00:00:24,014 --> 00:00:31,719
These are the twelve missions that should have been disasters — and the seconds, the switches, and the bent metal pins that decided otherwise.

8
00:00:32,866 --> 00:00:34,200
Twelve stories. In order.

9
00:00:34,200 --> 00:00:38,537
Each one closer to death than the last time you held your breath.

10
00:00:38,537 --> 00:00:41,539
This is every astronaut who almost died in space.

11
00:00:42,666 --> 00:00:45,748
Every astronaut who almost died in space.

12
00:00:46,866 --> 00:00:49,295
1962. The fireball.

13
00:00:50,433 --> 00:00:55,557
February 20th, 1962. John Glenn becomes the first American to orbit the Earth.

14
00:00:55,557 --> 00:00:59,892
Three orbits, four hours and fifty-five minutes. That was the plan.

15
00:00:59,892 --> 00:01:03,834
Then a single telemetry channel on the ground lit up.

16
00:01:04,966 --> 00:01:09,933
Segment 51. A signal saying Glenn's heat shield was no longer locked in place.

17
00:01:09,933 --> 00:01:15,964
If that was true, the only thing between him and 3,000 degrees of reentry plasma was loose.

18
00:01:17,100 --> 00:01:20,900
Mission control never told him directly. They just started asking strange questions.

19
00:01:20,900 --> 00:01:25,018
Is the landing bag switch off? Leave the retro pack on through reentry.

20
00:01:25,018 --> 00:01:29,769
Glenn was a test pilot. He did the math on what they were not saying.

21
00:01:30,900 --> 00:01:35,649
The straps of the retro pack would hold a loose shield in place. That was the theory.

22
00:01:35,649 --> 00:01:42,635
During reentry, Glenn watched flaming chunks stream past his window — and had no way to know if it was the retro pack burning away.

23
00:01:42,635 --> 00:01:46,547
Or his heat shield. He said, quote, I think the pack just let go.

24
00:01:47,666 --> 00:01:50,442
Four minutes and twenty seconds of radio blackout.

25
00:01:50,442 --> 00:01:53,565
Then Glenn's voice: that was a real fireball, boy.

26
00:01:53,565 --> 00:01:59,116
The signal had been false all along — a loose switch stem on the ground equipment.

27
00:01:59,116 --> 00:02:03,627
The shield was never loose. The terror was real. The danger never was.

28
00:02:04,766 --> 00:02:07,770
1965. The man outside.

29
00:02:08,900 --> 00:02:10,009
March 18th, 1965.

30
00:02:10,009 --> 00:02:16,298
Alexei Leonov floats out of an airlock and becomes the first human being to walk in space.

31
00:02:16,298 --> 00:02:20,368
Twelve minutes of history. Then he tried to come back inside.

32
00:02:21,500 --> 00:02:23,175
In vacuum, his suit had ballooned.

33
00:02:23,175 --> 00:02:28,200
It stiffened and swelled until his fingers pulled out of his gloves and his feet left his boots.

34
00:02:28,200 --> 00:02:31,271
He could not bend enough to fit back through the airlock.

35
00:02:31,271 --> 00:02:34,901
He was locked out of the only shelter within 200 kilometres straight down.

36
00:02:36,033 --> 00:02:38,258
So Leonov did the only thing left.

37
00:02:38,258 --> 00:02:48,428
He opened a valve and bled his own suit pressure down — from around 0.4 atmospheres toward 0.27 — trading the risk of the bends for a suit soft enough to bend.

38
00:02:48,428 --> 00:02:53,195
His core temperature spiked. He later said sweat sloshed inside the suit to his knees.

39
00:02:54,333 --> 00:02:59,934
He squeezed in head-first, against procedure, and got stuck sideways turning around to close the hatch.

40
00:02:59,934 --> 00:03:04,484
On live radio, the Soviet feed quietly went silent. He made it in.

41
00:03:04,484 --> 00:03:08,335
And Voskhod 2 was not done trying to kill its crew.

42
00:03:09,466 --> 00:03:11,434
The automatic reentry system failed.

43
00:03:11,434 --> 00:03:24,817
Commander Pavel Belyayev flew the retrofire by hand — the first manual reentry in Soviet history — and a 46 second delay getting back into their seats pushed the landing 386 kilometres off target.

44
00:03:24,817 --> 00:03:26,785
Into the taiga. In March.

45
00:03:27,933 --> 00:03:37,762
Two cosmonauts spent two nights in a forest at minus 30 degrees Celsius, in a capsule with a blown hatch, with a pistol between them because the taiga has bears and wolves in mating season.

46
00:03:37,762 --> 00:03:39,447
The rescue team arrived on skis.

47
00:03:39,447 --> 00:03:43,659
The first men to walk in space had to ski nine kilometres to go home.

48
00:03:44,800 --> 00:03:48,117
1966. One revolution per second.

49
00:03:49,266 --> 00:03:51,122
March 16th, 1966. Gemini 8.

50
00:03:51,122 --> 00:03:57,062
Neil Armstrong and David Scott have just performed the first docking of two spacecraft in history.

51
00:03:57,062 --> 00:04:03,372
Twenty-seven minutes later, out of contact with the ground, the stack begins to roll on its own.

52
00:04:04,500 --> 00:04:08,667
They undocked from the Agena, assuming the fault was on the target vehicle.

53
00:04:08,667 --> 00:04:10,591
It was the worst possible move.

54
00:04:10,591 --> 00:04:18,605
The problem was their own thruster — number 8, stuck firing — and without the Agena's mass, the little Gemini spun up like a flywheel.

55
00:04:19,733 --> 00:04:23,339
296 degrees per second. Nearly a full revolution every second.

56
00:04:23,339 --> 00:04:26,945
Their vision blurred and began to gray at the edges.

57
00:04:26,945 --> 00:04:32,715
Armstrong later said they were approaching the point where they could no longer read the instruments.

58
00:04:32,715 --> 00:04:36,321
After that comes blackout. After blackout, nobody flies the ship.

59
00:04:37,466 --> 00:04:42,631
Scott radioed a tracking ship: we have serious problems here. We're tumbling end over end.

60
00:04:42,631 --> 00:04:48,141
And Armstrong, half blind, reached above his head and found the reentry control switches by feel.

61
00:04:48,141 --> 00:04:52,617
The landing thrusters. The ones you are never supposed to touch in orbit.

62
00:04:53,766 --> 00:04:59,435
It worked. But the rule was absolute: touch the reentry system, and the mission ends now.

63
00:04:59,435 --> 00:05:10,772
Ten hours after launch, a three-day mission splashed down in the Pacific, 800 kilometres east of Okinawa, and two seasick astronauts bobbed in the swells for three hours waiting for a destroyer.

64
00:05:11,899 --> 00:05:15,142
Post-flight analysis found an electrical short. A single wire.

65
00:05:15,142 --> 00:05:22,348
Three years later, the man who out-flew it by feel, seconds from blackout, was the first human on the Moon.

66
00:05:23,466 --> 00:05:27,437
1969. Struck by lightning. Twice.

67
00:05:28,566 --> 00:05:32,695
November 14th, 1969. Apollo 12 lifts off into rain clouds.

68
00:05:32,695 --> 00:05:43,429
At 36 and a half seconds, the entire vehicle — the largest rocket ever flown, trailing a plume of ionized exhaust — becomes a lightning rod.

69
00:05:43,429 --> 00:05:45,493
And the sky uses it.

70
00:05:46,633 --> 00:05:52,074
Every warning light on the panel lit at once. All three fuel cells dropped offline.

71
00:05:52,074 --> 00:05:56,790
The guidance platform tumbled. Pete Conrad radioed: we just lost the platform, gang.

72
00:05:56,790 --> 00:05:59,692
We had everything in the world drop out.

73
00:05:59,692 --> 00:06:02,594
Sixteen seconds later, the lightning hit them again.

74
00:06:03,733 --> 00:06:07,354
In mission control, the telemetry screens turned to static garbage.

75
00:06:07,354 --> 00:06:10,250
Nobody knew what was happening inside the spacecraft.

76
00:06:10,250 --> 00:06:19,302
Nobody — except a 26-year-old controller named John Aaron, who had seen this exact garbage pattern once before, in an obscure test a year earlier.

77
00:06:20,433 --> 00:06:26,696
Flight, Eecom. Try S C E to auxiliary. The flight director had to ask him to repeat it.

78
00:06:26,696 --> 00:06:32,263
The capcom asked what it even was. In the capsule, Conrad didn't know the switch existed.

79
00:06:32,263 --> 00:06:37,831
Alan Bean found it, flipped it — and every screen in Houston came back to life.

80
00:06:38,966 --> 00:06:41,145
Here is the part they kept quiet.

81
00:06:41,145 --> 00:06:44,569
The lightning may have damaged the pyrotechnics that fire the parachutes.

82
00:06:44,569 --> 00:06:48,615
There was no way to check from orbit, and nothing anyone could do.

83
00:06:48,615 --> 00:06:57,330
So mission control said nothing — and let three men fly to the Moon, land on it, and fly home, possibly toward a 300-mile-per-hour impact with the ocean.

84
00:06:58,466 --> 00:07:00,927
On November 24th, all three parachutes opened.

85
00:07:00,927 --> 00:07:06,904
Conrad's verdict on the whole affair: I think we need to do a little more all-weather testing.

86
00:07:08,033 --> 00:07:10,541
1970. The explosion.

87
00:07:11,666 --> 00:07:15,818
You know Apollo 13. You know the explosion, 320,000 kilometres from Earth.

88
00:07:15,818 --> 00:07:30,004
What you may not know is that the bomb was installed before launch — an oxygen tank dropped five centimetres in 1968, then cooked in a ground test until the safety switches welded themselves shut and the wiring insulation burned away.

89
00:07:30,004 --> 00:07:31,388
It flew like that.

90
00:07:32,533 --> 00:07:37,344
Jack Swigert said it first: Okay Houston, we've had a problem here. Not we have.

91
00:07:37,344 --> 00:07:42,797
We've had. The side of the service module was already gone. Two of three fuel cells dead.

92
00:07:42,797 --> 00:07:46,326
Oxygen venting into space as a visible cloud around the ship.

93
00:07:47,466 --> 00:07:55,327
Three men moved into a lunar lander built to keep two men alive for two days — and asked it to keep three alive for four.

94
00:07:55,327 --> 00:07:57,443
The temperature fell to 3 degrees Celsius.

95
00:07:57,443 --> 00:08:01,676
Water was rationed to about 200 millilitres a day. Condensation ran down the walls.

96
00:08:01,676 --> 00:08:05,908
Fred Haise developed a fever from an infection his dehydrated body could not fight.

97
00:08:07,033 --> 00:08:09,688
Their own exhaled breath began to poison them.

98
00:08:09,688 --> 00:08:16,324
Carbon dioxide climbed toward the alarm line, and the lander's round filters could not take the command module's square cartridges.

99
00:08:16,324 --> 00:08:28,271
The fix that saved three lives was built from a flight-plan cover, plastic bags, a hose from a suit, and duct tape — designed on a table in Houston, read up by radio, line by line.

100
00:08:29,400 --> 00:08:41,265
With the guidance computer powered down, they aimed a 14 second engine burn by eye — Lovell holding the Earth's day-night line in the window crosshairs, Haise handling roll, Swigert counting the seconds on his wristwatch.

101
00:08:41,265 --> 00:08:44,891
A hand-flown course correction on the way home from the Moon.

102
00:08:46,033 --> 00:08:50,182
Reentry blackout was supposed to last four minutes. It lasted almost six.

103
00:08:50,182 --> 00:09:03,666
For 87 extra seconds, mission control sat in silence, knowing the heat shield lived inches from the tank that exploded — genuinely unsure whether they were waiting for a spacecraft or listening for three men who were already gone.

104
00:09:04,800 --> 00:09:12,457
Then three parachutes on the screens of Houston, and one of the most accurate splashdowns of the entire Apollo program.

105
00:09:12,457 --> 00:09:17,435
The cold had degraded one parachute pyro and a battery. Everything fired anyway.

106
00:09:17,435 --> 00:09:19,350
Barely counts as luck. Barely.

107
00:09:20,466 --> 00:09:23,783
1975. Twenty-one g.

108
00:09:24,933 --> 00:09:26,010
April 5th, 1975.

109
00:09:26,010 --> 00:09:36,786
Vasily Lazarev and Oleg Makarov are riding a Soyuz toward the Salyut 4 station when the staging fails — the third stage lights while still bolted to the stage below.

110
00:09:36,786 --> 00:09:40,377
The rocket pitches off course at the edge of space.

111
00:09:40,377 --> 00:09:43,610
The abort system fires at roughly 150 kilometres up.

112
00:09:44,733 --> 00:09:48,779
What followed was the steepest ride home in the history of spaceflight.

113
00:09:48,779 --> 00:09:54,848
The capsule fell into a ballistic dive and the deceleration climbed past anything the vehicle was designed for.

114
00:09:54,848 --> 00:09:56,871
Twenty-one times the force of gravity.

115
00:09:56,871 --> 00:10:00,917
Their vision drained to black and white, then closed to a tunnel.

116
00:10:00,917 --> 00:10:02,940
Lazarev said breathing became nearly impossible.

117
00:10:04,066 --> 00:10:08,852
They came down on a snow-covered mountainside in the Altai — and started rolling.

118
00:10:08,852 --> 00:10:09,194
Downhill.

119
00:10:09,194 --> 00:10:13,980
The parachute snagged on vegetation and held, arresting the capsule short of a drop.

120
00:10:13,980 --> 00:10:17,057
The two men inside had just survived 21 g.

121
00:10:17,057 --> 00:10:19,792
The mountain almost finished what the rocket started.

122
00:10:20,933 --> 00:10:29,131
Convinced they might have crossed into China — a hostile border in 1975 — Lazarev burned classified documents in the snow.

123
00:10:29,131 --> 00:10:32,645
They were actually hundreds of kilometres inside Soviet territory.

124
00:10:32,645 --> 00:10:35,377
They spent the night on the mountain.

125
00:10:35,377 --> 00:10:38,891
Lazarev's injuries from the g-load ended his flying career.

126
00:10:38,891 --> 00:10:42,014
The Soviet Union acknowledged only an unnamed anomaly.

127
00:10:43,133 --> 00:10:46,842
1983. The only pad abort in history.

128
00:10:47,966 --> 00:10:49,103
September 26th, 1983.

129
00:10:49,103 --> 00:11:01,981
Vladimir Titov and Gennady Strekalov are strapped on top of a fully fueled Soyuz at Baikonur, 90 seconds from launch, when a valve fails and kerosene begins pouring across the base of the rocket.

130
00:11:01,981 --> 00:11:06,905
It ignites. The rocket is on fire. And the crew has no idea.

131
00:11:08,033 --> 00:11:12,120
Their portholes faced away from the flames. They felt vibrations, nothing more.

132
00:11:12,120 --> 00:11:18,251
And the abort could not fire — because the fire had already burned through the abort control cables.

133
00:11:18,251 --> 00:11:25,744
The escape command had to travel by radio, from two operators, in two separate rooms, transmitting within five seconds of each other.

134
00:11:26,866 --> 00:11:33,579
For roughly ten seconds, two human beings sat on 300 tonnes of burning propellant while ground crews raced the fire.

135
00:11:33,579 --> 00:11:38,278
Then the escape tower fired: 14 to 17 g, straight up, for five seconds.

136
00:11:38,278 --> 00:11:44,655
Six seconds after they left, the rocket collapsed into a fireball that burned on the pad for 20 hours.

137
00:11:45,800 --> 00:11:47,675
The capsule landed four kilometres away.

138
00:11:47,675 --> 00:11:52,676
The crew recorder caught them swearing the entire way up — Titov admitted that much himself.

139
00:11:52,676 --> 00:11:57,365
On the ground, shaken and bruised, the two men asked the rescue crews for cigarettes.

140
00:11:57,365 --> 00:11:58,927
Then someone handed them vodka.

141
00:11:58,927 --> 00:12:03,929
It remains the only time in history a crew has escaped a rocket on the pad.

142
00:12:05,066 --> 00:12:07,495
1988. We are going to die.

143
00:12:08,633 --> 00:12:10,367
December 2nd, 1988. Atlantis.

144
00:12:10,367 --> 00:12:17,303
The second flight after Challenger, and a mission so classified its cargo is still officially unconfirmed.

145
00:12:17,303 --> 00:12:24,672
Eighty-five seconds up, insulation from a booster nose cap breaks free and sandblasts the orbiter's right side.

146
00:12:25,800 --> 00:12:31,394
In orbit, commander Hoot Gibson steered the robot arm's camera under the belly and went quiet.

147
00:12:31,394 --> 00:12:35,591
Seven hundred and seven damage sites. Whole gouges through the heat tiles.

148
00:12:35,591 --> 00:12:41,186
One tile missing entirely. Gibson looked at the image and thought: we are going to die.

149
00:12:42,333 --> 00:12:47,482
Because the mission was classified, the video went to Houston encrypted, in degraded slow-scan frames.

150
00:12:47,482 --> 00:12:51,944
Engineers looked at the mush and called the damage lights and shadows. Acceptable.

151
00:12:51,944 --> 00:12:57,093
The crew, looking at the real thing out the window, was told it was fine.

152
00:12:57,093 --> 00:13:01,899
There was no rescue option. There was no repair kit. There was only reentry.

153
00:13:03,033 --> 00:13:06,381
Gibson told his crew there was no use dying all tensed up.

154
00:13:06,381 --> 00:13:15,310
And he made himself a private promise: if the wing began to burn through, in his last seconds, he was going to tell mission control exactly what he thought of their analysis.

155
00:13:16,433 --> 00:13:21,584
Atlantis came home with the worst tile damage of any shuttle that ever landed.

156
00:13:21,584 --> 00:13:32,621
The missing tile — the one gap that mattered — happened to sit over a thick metal antenna mounting plate, which took the heat and half-melted instead of the airframe.

157
00:13:32,621 --> 00:13:39,612
Fifteen years later, the same institutional blindness — damage dismissed from degraded imagery — killed the crew of Columbia.

158
00:13:39,612 --> 00:13:42,555
In 1988, one metal plate held the line.

159
00:13:43,700 --> 00:13:46,129
1997. Fire on Mir.

160
00:13:47,266 --> 00:13:48,505
February 23rd, 1997.

161
00:13:48,505 --> 00:13:56,351
The station Mir is overcrowded — six people aboard during a crew handover, and only two three-seat Soyuz lifeboats.

162
00:13:56,351 --> 00:14:01,307
To make enough air for six, the crew burns solid-fuel oxygen canisters.

163
00:14:01,307 --> 00:14:06,675
Aleksandr Lazutkin ignites one in the Kvant module. It does not burn normally.

164
00:14:07,800 --> 00:14:17,825
A blowtorch flame nearly a metre long erupts from the canister, spraying sparks and droplets of molten metal at the opposite wall — the wall between the crew and vacuum.

165
00:14:17,825 --> 00:14:20,164
Smoke fills the entire station in seconds.

166
00:14:20,164 --> 00:14:25,511
Jerry Linenger, NASA's doctor aboard, said he could not see his own fingers at arm's length.

167
00:14:26,633 --> 00:14:32,102
Linenger grabbed a respirator, pulled it on, inhaled — and the mask collapsed against his face.

168
00:14:32,102 --> 00:14:35,520
Empty. A dead unit, in a station full of smoke.

169
00:14:35,520 --> 00:14:38,596
He found a second one before he lost consciousness.

170
00:14:38,596 --> 00:14:45,774
And here is the geometry that mattered: the fire sat directly across the passage to one of the two escape craft.

171
00:14:45,774 --> 00:14:50,901
If the order came to abandon Mir, three of the six had no way home.

172
00:14:52,033 --> 00:14:55,706
You cannot smother a fire that manufactures its own oxygen.

173
00:14:55,706 --> 00:15:04,154
Commander Valery Korzun sprayed foam while Linenger physically braced him against the recoil, and the crew waited for the canister to exhaust itself.

174
00:15:04,154 --> 00:15:08,562
Then the argument started. Moscow's official report: the fire lasted 90 seconds.

175
00:15:08,562 --> 00:15:15,909
Linenger, the American who fought it, timed it at 14 minutes — and says he was told to keep quiet.

176
00:15:17,033 --> 00:15:20,394
No one died. No one was even seriously hurt.

177
00:15:20,394 --> 00:15:28,237
And four months later, the same station, with some of the same crew, would come within minutes of killing everyone aboard.

178
00:15:28,237 --> 00:15:30,852
Because 1997 on Mir was not done.

179
00:15:32,000 --> 00:15:34,586
1997. The collision.

180
00:15:35,733 --> 00:15:36,800
June 25th, 1997.

181
00:15:36,800 --> 00:15:47,472
To save money, Moscow wants to prove it can dock a Progress cargo ship without the expensive automatic radar — so the test is flown with the radar switched off.

182
00:15:47,472 --> 00:15:59,922
Commander Vasily Tsibliyev is hand-flying seven tonnes of spacecraft by joystick, watching a TV screen, with no range data, no speed data, and a laser rangefinder that can't find the target against the Earth below.

183
00:16:01,066 --> 00:16:07,701
By the time the crew sees the Progress clearly, it is too close, too fast, and it does not stop.

184
00:16:07,701 --> 00:16:12,678
It clips a solar array and slams into the Spektr module at roughly walking pace.

185
00:16:12,678 --> 00:16:17,654
Seven tonnes at walking pace is enough. The hull cracks. And the crew's ears pop.

186
00:16:18,800 --> 00:16:24,452
That pressure drop in their ears was the station bleeding air into space, with a hiss they could hear.

187
00:16:24,452 --> 00:16:28,023
The crew did the arithmetic: under half an hour of breathable margin.

188
00:16:28,023 --> 00:16:36,354
And they could not seal the leaking module — because 18 power and data cables ran through the open hatchway, and the hatch could not close across them.

189
00:16:37,500 --> 00:16:44,296
So Lazutkin and Michael Foale ripped them out one by one — and cut the last two with a knife — while the hiss continued.

190
00:16:44,296 --> 00:16:50,822
They wrestled a hatch cover into the opening with minutes of margin left, and the escaping pressure sucked it shut and held it there.

191
00:16:51,966 --> 00:17:02,111
Sealing Spektr cost them its solar power, and the impact had set the whole station tumbling — arrays off the sun, systems dying, Mir going dark and silent.

192
00:17:02,111 --> 00:17:13,704
Foale then estimated the station's spin by holding his thumb against a window and timing the stars drifting past it, and radioed the numbers so the Soyuz thrusters could stop the tumble.

193
00:17:13,704 --> 00:17:16,965
Celestial navigation, by thumb, to save a space station.

194
00:17:18,100 --> 00:17:21,261
2013. Drowning in a spacesuit.

195
00:17:22,400 --> 00:17:23,539
July 16th, 2013.

196
00:17:23,539 --> 00:17:32,277
Luca Parmitano is 44 minutes into a spacewalk outside the International Space Station when he feels water on the back of his head.

197
00:17:32,277 --> 00:17:37,976
Inside a sealed helmet, in vacuum, there is exactly one direction water can go: nowhere.

198
00:17:37,976 --> 00:17:39,876
It clings, and it crawls.

199
00:17:41,000 --> 00:17:45,248
A clogged filter is quietly pumping his suit's cooling water into his air vent.

200
00:17:45,248 --> 00:17:51,620
In zero gravity the blob wraps his head like a living thing — over his ears, so the radio goes muffled.

201
00:17:51,620 --> 00:17:52,834
Then over his eyes.

202
00:17:52,834 --> 00:17:58,296
A week earlier, drops of water in the same suit had been dismissed as a leaking drink bag.

203
00:17:58,296 --> 00:17:59,207
Nobody had investigated.

204
00:18:00,333 --> 00:18:01,711
Houston aborts the spacewalk.

205
00:18:01,711 --> 00:18:06,877
As Parmitano turns back, the station crosses into orbital night and the sun goes out.

206
00:18:06,877 --> 00:18:18,932
He is now blind, half deaf, water creeping toward his nose — calculating, by his own account, whether he could open his helmet's purge valve and survive the escaping air if he started to drown.

207
00:18:20,066 --> 00:18:28,110
He navigated back by memory and by the feel of his safety tether reeling in — hand over hand along a station he could not see.

208
00:18:28,110 --> 00:18:32,751
Chris Cassidy followed him in; the crew tore his helmet off the moment pressure allowed.

209
00:18:32,751 --> 00:18:36,154
A litre and a half of water came out with it.

210
00:18:36,154 --> 00:18:39,867
NASA's own report used the words: a condition that was life threatening.

211
00:18:41,000 --> 00:18:48,158
Every spacesuit that has gone outside since carries an absorbent pad behind the head and a snorkel running down to the chest.

212
00:18:48,158 --> 00:18:52,389
Because one man nearly became the first human being to drown in space.

213
00:18:53,533 --> 00:18:55,806
2018. A short flight.

214
00:18:56,933 --> 00:19:01,319
October 11th, 2018. Nick Hague's first launch. Alexey Ovchinin's second.

215
00:19:01,319 --> 00:19:09,213
Two minutes up, a strap-on booster fails to separate cleanly, swings back, and strikes the rocket's core stage.

216
00:19:09,213 --> 00:19:13,599
Fifty kilometres above Kazakhstan, the vehicle begins to break apart.

217
00:19:14,733 --> 00:19:18,664
The abort system fires and rips the capsule free.

218
00:19:18,664 --> 00:19:24,779
Inside: violent side-to-side shaking, the master alarm, and sudden weightlessness where thrust should be.

219
00:19:24,779 --> 00:19:30,457
Ovchinin's entire commentary to the ground, flat calm: an accident with the booster.

220
00:19:30,457 --> 00:19:33,515
And then: that was a short flight.

221
00:19:34,633 --> 00:19:44,484
They fell back to Earth on a ballistic arc, pulling nearly 7 g, and landed 400 kilometres from the pad — 19 minutes and 41 seconds after liftoff.

222
00:19:44,484 --> 00:19:47,299
Rescue crews found them upright, bruised, and smiling.

223
00:19:47,299 --> 00:19:52,928
The cause: a sensor pin, bent by a few degrees during assembly on the pad floor.

224
00:19:52,928 --> 00:19:57,150
A technician's hands nearly killed two men at the edge of space.

225
00:19:58,300 --> 00:20:06,370
Five months later, both men strapped into another Soyuz on the same launch pad, and flew to the station like nothing had happened.

226
00:20:06,370 --> 00:20:08,827
Because that is the actual job description.

227
00:20:09,966 --> 00:20:10,642
Twelve missions.

228
00:20:10,642 --> 00:20:25,848
Thirty-two people who by every reasonable calculation should be names on a memorial — saved by a retro pack strap, a metal plate, a knife, a wristwatch, a thumb against a window, and controllers who recognized one pattern of static in a wall of noise.

229
00:20:26,966 --> 00:20:36,492
Every fix on this list — the snorkel in the suit, the lightning rules, the launch escape tests — exists because someone almost died and lived to file the report.

230
00:20:36,492 --> 00:20:41,255
Spaceflight is not safe. It is merely paid for, one near miss at a time.

231
00:20:42,400 --> 00:20:45,093
But 21 people never got to file that report.

232
00:20:45,093 --> 00:20:54,668
If these twelve survivals held your breath, the stories of the ones who never came home will stay with you far longer — that full documentary is on your screen right now.

233
00:20:54,668 --> 00:20:55,566
Subscribe to mindwired.

